<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lin Biao</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lin Yurong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lin Biao was a Chinese military commander and politician, ranked third among the Ten Marshals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. Pivotal to the Communist victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a>, he commanded the Liaoshen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pingjin-campaign">Pingjin campaigns</a> and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. Minister of National Defense from 1959, he directed the compilation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations-from-chairman-mao-tse-tung">Quotations from Chairman Mao Zedong</a>, the Little Red Book, and helped build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s cult of personality; in 1966 he was designated Mao's sole successor. He died in 1971 when his aircraft crashed near Öndörkhaan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, in circumstances that remain disputed, and was posthumously condemned as a traitor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lin-biao.jpg" alt="Lin Biao" width="330" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linbiao2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The May Fourth Movement of 1919 attacked it, and in the People’s Republic of China the campaign to “criticise <b>Lin Biao</b> and criticise Confucius” in the 1970s denounced it.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lin Biao</b>’s cult of Mao’s charisma, “the atom bomb of the spirit”, became the ruling interpretation; from 1967 Mao and the army began to restrain the mass organisations.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Its title phrase appeared on the cover of a junior middle school textbook on Mao Zedong Thought in the 1960s, and <b>Lin Biao</b> used the song's words in an inscription; according to Zhou Enlai, the title came from a slogan of Lin Biao.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</a></li></ul></section>
